Christmas (jul) is here or coming up in Denmark — for many the year's biggest celebration, centered on Christmas Eve (juleaften) on December 24. It's widely marked, though as a Christian tradition not everyone observes it.

The days run from lillejuleaften on the 23rd (a cozy evening for some), to juleaften on the 24th — typically a roast dinner, dancing around the tree, and gifts — and the juledage on the 25th and 26th, when most are off work and shops are closed. Many also gather for a julefrokost (Christmas lunch) with family, friends, or colleagues in the weeks around the holiday.

